## What happened

**Apple's security frameworks (Security.framework / CryptoKit) have never supported DSA.** In earlier .NET versions, the runtime shipped an OpenSSL compatibility shim on macOS to fill this gap. Microsoft has been deprecating DSA since .NET 7 (`SYSLIB0028` obsolescence warning), and in .NET 11 the OpenSSL shim for DSA on macOS was removed entirely. `DSA.Create()` now throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on Apple platforms.

## How to fix it

You have two options, depending on whether you need to keep verifying legacy DSA signatures or can migrate:

### Option 1 — Use a managed DSA implementation (verify-only, no native dependency)

Use **BouncyCastle** (`Portable.BouncyCastle` or `BouncyCastle.Cryptography`), which implements DSA in pure managed C# and works everywhere:

```csharp
// dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto.Parameters;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

public static bool VerifyLegacyDsaSignature(byte[] data, byte[] signature, DsaPublicKeyParameters publicKey)
{
    var signer = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA1withDSA");
    signer.Init(forSigning: false, publicKey);
    signer.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
    return signer.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

This is the best path for **legacy verification only** — no platform-specific dependencies, works on all targets.

### Option 2 — Migrate to a modern algorithm

If you control both signing and verification, migrate to **ECDSA** (or RSA), which is supported natively on all platforms:

```csharp
using var ecdsa = ECDsa.Create(ECCurve.NamedCurves.nistP256);
byte[] sig = ecdsa.SignData(data, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
bool valid = ecdsa.VerifyData(data, sig, HashAlgorithmName.SHA256);
```

## Recommendation

For your case (verifying **legacy** DSA signatures), go with **Option 1**. You can't re-sign old artifacts, so you need a DSA implementation — BouncyCastle provides one with zero native dependencies. Long-term, plan to migrate new signatures to ECDSA.
